WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.295 --> 00:00:05.920
SUZANNE FREY: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.800
My name is Suzanne Frey.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.520
I'm responsible for protecting
the data our users entrust

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.760
to Google, and
specifically, to G Suite.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.670
Now, our users include large,
heavily regulated enterprise

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.054
customers, say, in
financial services,

00:00:25.054 --> 00:00:26.470
small businesses
that could become

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.200
the next big thing,
large national government

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:35.780
agencies, small local agencies,
education, institutions,

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.160
nonprofits, and the like,
across multiple jurisdictions,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.960
regulatory requirements, and
locales all over the world.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.350
Now, those users have
trusted their most sensitive

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:52.020
information to us.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.540
It's a responsibility
that myself

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.870
and the thousands of other
individuals in Google Cloud

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.230
take very seriously.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.180
I'm going to show
a lot of words,

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.920
I'm going to share a lot
of things we do today,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.400
but frankly, at
the end of the day,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.486
it's our actions that matter.

00:01:08.486 --> 00:01:10.110
And every single day,
I'm proud to work

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.360
alongside these
thousands of Googlers who

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.340
take the security and the trust
our users have entrusted to us

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.920
very, very seriously.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.070
So let's talk about
that concept of trust.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.750
Why do we trust?

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.120
Actually, most
people don't think

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.950
about their fundamental internal
trust algorithm very much.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.740
It's only when we're
faced with something new

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.530
that that actually
percolates to the surface.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.240
Fundamentally, our
trust algorithms

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.820
are formed from the
moment we're born.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.170
We experience something new.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.730
We're vulnerable to something we
don't know, whether it's food,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.890
you know, or an
experience or a location.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.610
And we either have a positive,
negative, or neutral experience

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.130
with each one of
those endeavors.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.530
And over time, we
learn to understand

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.840
what we're going to trust.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.700
And we tend to
trust the things we

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.539
know because we've collected
data on them as human beings,

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:11.140
right?

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.120
And we trust what
we know until what

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.180
we know we can no longer trust.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.640
So let's sort of back up
and think about the concept

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.730
of on-premise solutions.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:24.160
And on premise, you're
not really vulnerable

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.350
or perceived not to be
vulnerable because you're

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.390
perceived to be in control.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:31.120
I think many people
have heard the analogy

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.460
that on-premise solutions
are like sticking

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:36.980
your money under the mattress.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.570
You're in control.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:40.070
You know where
you put the money,

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.440
you know how much
money there is,

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.630
and if you need to go get
it, you know where to go.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:52.300
Now, trust here
is because you've

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.220
gotten used to that, right?

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.320
You've become accustomed to it.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.050
Nothing bad has happened,
and therefore, you're

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.800
comfortable with it.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.434
And I was thinking
a little bit today

00:03:02.434 --> 00:03:03.850
during the keynote
where customers

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.340
were talking about going
through the change management

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.880
process of moving to cloud,
and how easy that was.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.360
Fundamentally, change
management, at its core,

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.250
is trust management.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.530
It's actually getting
customers across that hoop

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.960
of going through
a new experience

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.237
and landing in a
positive and happy space.

00:03:22.237 --> 00:03:24.070
That's something I think
that differentiates

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.150
Google very, very well.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.670
That we are able to take
customers on that journey

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.640
expeditiously,
safely, and at scale.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.710
Because any of you who
have taken an organization

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.170
through a massive change
management exercise

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.580
know that's not easy.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.982
And fundamentally,
that's about trust.

00:03:39.982 --> 00:03:41.440
Now, I said under
the circumstance,

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:45.670
you know, put the money under
the mattress, know where it is,

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:49.600
and you tend to believe that
that's the right way to operate

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.420
until it no longer is, right?

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.690
Because fundamentally,
many companies

00:03:55.690 --> 00:04:00.460
today are realizing that scaling
to meet the demands of security

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:04.660
on an on-premise solution
is becoming an exceptionally

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.700
challenging game.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.820
And if adversaries
happen to know

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.820
you are running your
operations on premise,

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:18.250
it is extremely easy for them
to break those operations.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:26.050
So changes like movement to the
cloud-- they're not new, right?

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:28.794
Over time, customers went
from storing their money

00:04:28.794 --> 00:04:30.460
under the mattress
to actually putting--

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.620
or I would say, individuals--
to actually putting

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.780
their money in a bank, right?

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:37.990
Something as important as
one's financial assets--

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:42.520
actually transferring that
trust to someone else to manage.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.090
That happened over time,
banks made improvements,

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.740
and now many people
would say you're

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:51.760
absolutely silly to put your
money under the mattress.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.490
Same thing with airlines.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.560
Many, many companies didn't
want to use airlines,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.935
or they ran their own until
they realized that the safety

00:04:58.935 --> 00:05:01.270
and security is best
entrusted to someone who

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.820
does that as their business.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.570
Electricity-- do you even
think twice about the fact

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.250
that your electricity
is probably

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:10.840
provided by a third party?

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.180
Kudos to all of you that are
doing solar and going green.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.600
But for the most
part, if your company

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:20.170
didn't have electricity,
you wouldn't be running.

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:23.200
Yet people don't think about
that from a trust perspective

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:26.050
very much anymore until
there's a power outage.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.990
And then, obviously, we
all think about it a lot.

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:30.880
These are not new trends.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.610
This is where we are
with the cloud today.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.340
We're at that precipice of sort
of going from the early adopter

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.560
phase to the late
adopters realizing

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.580
that those who do
cloud do security,

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.120
do fundamentally a full stack
of operations around security

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:49.330
and privacy, better than
individual on-premise operators

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:52.544
can possibly do today.

00:05:52.544 --> 00:05:55.360
So I don't know
if somebody wants

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:56.920
to shout out our number here--

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.912
intimate setting.

00:05:58.912 --> 00:06:00.370
What percentage of
companies do you

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:02.536
think have migrated some
portion of their operations

00:06:02.536 --> 00:06:05.146
to the cloud?

00:06:05.146 --> 00:06:07.525
I heard 80%.

00:06:07.525 --> 00:06:08.025
96%.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.840
It's actually 44%.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.595
That's just some portion
of their operations.

00:06:16.595 --> 00:06:18.220
That number's gone
up dramatically year

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:21.110
on year in the last three years.

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:23.839
How many companies have
migrated all of their operations

00:06:23.839 --> 00:06:24.380
to the cloud?

00:06:27.642 --> 00:06:28.310
I heard 1%.

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:32.010
5%.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.578
The number is 8%.

00:06:34.578 --> 00:06:35.514
Around 8%.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:41.332
There's a huge upside
in that number.

00:06:41.332 --> 00:06:42.790
And again, we're
seeing this number

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.810
grow very quickly, as well.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:51.230
The cloud really matters, so
this was an interesting stat

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:54.850
I was presenting to some
CSOs a few weeks ago,

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:57.420
and was talking about, sort
of, presentations to the board

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.130
in the cloud and the like.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.499
And you know, what are
some compelling things

00:07:01.499 --> 00:07:02.290
that are happening?

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:04.380
So 20 years ago,
the average time

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.070
a company would spend on
the S&amp;P 500 was 14 years.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:16.150
Fast forward to the last five
years and the moving average,

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:18.590
and it's seven years.

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:23.000
Average time that a company will
actually spend in the S&amp;P 500.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.690
S&amp;P 500 is a mixture of
public and private companies,

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.814
based on valuation.

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:30.480
Now, what's making
the difference there?

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.100
Well, there's a few things.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.580
Highlighted most
dramatically here

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:36.690
are breakthroughs
in cloud computing,

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:42.040
and companies' adoption
of cloud computing.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:46.170
It allows companies to
focus on what they do best.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:49.160
So I'm going to name a few
companies here, all of whom

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:54.170
are great companies, but I
want to talk about valuation.

00:07:54.170 --> 00:08:01.170
Airbnb as of Q2 2016 was
valued higher than Marriott.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:05.240
Uber was valued
higher than Fedex.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:09.510
All great companies, but
the higher-valued companies

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:13.440
were actually
cloud-based companies.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.630
They were born in the
cloud, operate their tech

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:18.360
in the cloud, and
they're able to move

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.300
more quickly as a
result. They inherit

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.260
the massive, massive, sort
of, security and innovation

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:27.356
benefits of being in
the cloud as opposed

00:08:27.356 --> 00:08:28.980
to those who have to
work through a lot

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:31.320
of different parties
to get done what they

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:32.690
need to get done on premise.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:36.610
And even adoption of tech.

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:40.510
So look at some key
cloud providers here.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:43.539
You know, it's gotten much,
much more accelerated.

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:48.820
Box was 39 months, actually,
to reach $10 million

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:50.470
in subscription.

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:54.100
Fast forward to Slack
in 2014, and we're

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.960
looking at 12-ish months--
a little less than 12 months

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:58.090
to the first $10 million.

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:01.730
And these curves keep repeating
themselves with new companies.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:04.510
It's pretty awesome.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.130
So in short, more
and more companies

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:08.140
are trusting the cloud,
and they are seeing

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:09.790
the benefits of the cloud.

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:13.190
Now, I'm talking right now about
valuation and things like that,

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:16.090
but I want to get into the
nitty gritty details of what

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:20.800
differentiates Google's cloud
and G Suite in protecting

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.005
your data.

00:09:23.005 --> 00:09:24.630
We're particularly
proud of this quote.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.255
Last year at RSA--
and I think a number

00:09:26.255 --> 00:09:30.810
of folks, maybe, in this room
went to RSA this year, as well.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:33.030
But the statement-- and
I've got to read it--

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.760
"CSOs would be wise to follow
Google's security operations

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:38.550
and strategies because the
company's infrastructure

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:42.440
is years ahead of
most enterprises."

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.510
Particularly proud
of that quote.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.120
I happen to agree, and
I'm going to tell you

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:51.150
now a little bit more
about why this is the case.

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:53.690
So why our cloud is
the trusted choice.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:57.410
Three messages I want
folks to take home today.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:09:59.540
First is defense-in-depth.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:01.250
And I'm going to talk
about the problem

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:04.640
of the
vendor-in-the-middle, VITM,

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:09.860
and what we did early on at
Google's birth to address

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:12.950
the security challenges
of having multiple vendors

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:14.186
in the mix.

00:10:14.186 --> 00:10:15.560
Secondly, I'm
going to talk about

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:17.750
our security-first culture.

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:20.050
And all things breed
from that culture--

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:24.470
our innovations, the people
who protect what we do,

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.780
and the technology,
even the community

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:32.000
that we engage outside of Google
has its roots in our culture.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.482
And last but not least is
our strong track record.

00:10:34.482 --> 00:10:36.440
I'm going to talk about
how we prove that track

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:39.880
record and engage,
again, our users

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:45.324
and retain them from the
moment that they join us.

00:10:45.324 --> 00:10:46.990
So let's talk about
the defense-in-depth

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:50.200
and the vendor-in-the-middle
problem.

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:52.330
When I first joined
Google, I actually

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:54.010
ran enterprise
operations on site.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:55.840
I was an on-premise person.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:59.300
This was a little
over 10 years ago.

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:02.410
And I worked on
technical infrastructure.

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:03.970
And I was like,
this crazy company

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:06.310
is building its own servers.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:07.030
Like wow.

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:08.530
I mean, why are
you doing that when

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:11.470
you can just go outsource
and buy, and just buy in bulk

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:13.540
and get a great deal?

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:16.510
Wasn't too long until I
realized this is genius.

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:19.180
And we were the only company
doing this at the time.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:25.140
And fundamentally, once
we start at the bottom

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:27.810
of that stack, our
technical infrastructure,

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:30.700
everything in that stack builds
up all the way through a G

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:36.340
Suite to provide a full
stack of security ownership

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:38.225
that is unrivaled
in the industry.

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:44.750
So let's start
with data centers.

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:49.490
We design, build,
operate our data centers.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:51.920
And a lot of the stuff that's
in the news folks know.

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:55.190
We run highly efficient data
centers, highly green data

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:56.780
centers.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:12:00.860
But we've also designed
for security from day one.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:05.150
So access to the data center
floor, actual physical access

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:06.832
to the site--

00:12:06.832 --> 00:12:08.540
they've all been part
of what we've done.

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:10.490
And the fact that
we own and operate

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:13.520
and Googlers own and
operate these sites

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:17.180
is fundamental to making sure
that from the very start,

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:21.820
anything that gets stored in
there is going to be protected.

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:23.290
We announced last
year we started

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.850
to design our own chips--

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:28.420
another moment where some
people said, you're crazy.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:31.030
But from a security standpoint
and owning everything

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:35.650
from the ground up,
it's a very smart move.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:36.840
Design our own servers--

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.260
heck, we design our own racks.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:42.040
We design and build our own
racks, networking equipment,

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:42.790
and the like.

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:47.470
Again, we're eliminating
the vendors in the middle.

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:50.260
If you think about
on-premise, the number

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:54.370
of vendors managed
on premise typically

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:56.650
is a very high number.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:58.630
You have no way of
understanding the collision

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:01.630
between those vendors, no way
of understanding and managing

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:04.749
and even controlling when's the
patch coming for this problem?

00:13:04.749 --> 00:13:06.040
When are you going to fix that?

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:08.980
You often maybe even have a
strategic outsourcing vendor

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:11.690
managing those vendors.

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:14.020
Here, it's Google
and our customer,

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:16.450
and we are accountable
for making sure

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:18.730
that everything in
that stack is secure,

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:21.610
and we are empowered to fix it.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:25.930
And that empowerment to fix it
differentiates us very strongly

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.130
from many other providers.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.110
Furthermore, Diane
mentioned this morning

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:33.870
that we were probably, possibly,
the largest network provider

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.330
in the world.

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:37.900
And I'll get into
some stats on this,

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:40.560
so I'll do that
in just a second.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.470
But that network capacity
allows us to route,

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:47.090
to prevent denial
of service attacks,

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:50.950
and to allow our customers
to scale very quickly.

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:53.932
So you have the security of
knowing the capacity is going

00:13:53.932 --> 00:13:55.140
to be there when you need it.

00:13:57.910 --> 00:14:00.370
We write our own
application services.

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:02.050
We write our firmware.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:05.590
We write the services that
handle storage and job

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:07.100
scheduling.

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:10.000
We write our own code control
system and release management

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.660
systems, and I'll talk about
those in a moment, as well.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:15.850
We don't use those
third party systems

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:18.400
because, again, it
would disempower us

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:21.640
from owning the quick and
expeditious resolution

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:25.340
of any issue that occurs.

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:29.140
And lastly, we build
our own devices.

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:32.040
This isn't to say you
can't use your own devices.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.280
We're actually being very,
very open about the ecosystem.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:38.570
But something like a Chromebook
is a clear differentiator

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:40.250
in our solution offering.

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:41.630
It brings the
power of Chrome, it

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:43.820
brings the power cloud,
and it [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:46.880
the fact that Google is
responsible for ensuring

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:50.510
you are secure, happy, and
delighted with your experience

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:51.110
with us.

00:14:54.185 --> 00:14:55.560
I mentioned on
the server front--

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:57.184
I just want to talk
about a few things.

00:14:57.184 --> 00:15:01.190
Again, servers are optimized
for a global fleet operation.

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:03.330
We don't put any sort
of unnecessary hardware,

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:04.350
even, on those.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:05.850
And folks can go
into depth and look

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:08.370
online at what we do there.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:09.360
Verified boot.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:11.100
Again, a server will
not boot unless it

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:14.830
goes through extensive
amount of tests.

00:15:14.830 --> 00:15:17.610
Hardened operating system and,
again, secured applications,

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:19.140
job scheduling and the like--

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:21.090
all of these things go
through rigorous checks

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:24.390
before they can even run in
the production environment.

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:26.610
And for example, I'll
tell you with, like,

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:29.390
even retirement of solutions.

00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:31.560
Disk erase at scale.

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.500
We're going to bring down our
end-of-life a given server--

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.000
the process by which
we check, double-check,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:41.820
triple-check a disk has been
erased before we crush it.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:46.110
It's a pretty, pretty robust
and extensive operation.

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:51.270
We recognize that the importance
of protecting our customer's

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:52.720
privacy is paramount.

00:15:56.910 --> 00:16:00.770
77 points of presence,
33 countries,

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:02.280
and a robust undersea network.

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:03.821
We were the first
provider, actually,

00:16:03.821 --> 00:16:06.410
to engage in partnerships
in undersea network cables.

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:08.339
We've now set a trend
for the industry.

00:16:08.339 --> 00:16:10.880
We're bring the internet to the
world in places of the world,

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:13.280
and connecting undersea
cables where there has

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:15.530
been no capacity previously.

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:18.950
Again, having a key stake
in those undersea cables

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:23.040
gives us the ability to route,
reroute, and bring the internet

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:24.760
all over, to any user.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.991
And for a multinational company,
this is extremely important,

00:16:27.991 --> 00:16:28.490
as well.

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:34.040
So now I'm going
to talk a bit about

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:36.244
our security-first culture.

00:16:36.244 --> 00:16:37.160
There's a lot in here.

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:39.201
And here, you're going to
get more of the laundry

00:16:39.201 --> 00:16:41.910
list of features that are
in G Suite and beyond,

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:44.230
and a little bit more about
the people behind this.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:49.560
So security-first.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:51.810
I mentioned that there
are thousands of engineers

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:55.320
in Google Cloud, and
all of those individuals

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:57.810
go through training
around how to handle

00:16:57.810 --> 00:16:59.820
user data and the like.

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:03.180
But out of those
thousands, we have 700 plus

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:06.710
dedicated security
engineers that

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:10.255
are protecting our customer's
information at scale, right?

00:17:10.255 --> 00:17:12.380
Coming up with the latest
and greatest innovations,

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:14.900
understanding how
adversaries are behaving,

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:17.030
building scaled and
automated processes

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:19.302
to protect and detect.

00:17:19.302 --> 00:17:22.579
To detect and protect.

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:26.869
We've published over 160
security research papers.

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:28.940
Last month, for
example, some folks

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:33.650
may have read about breaking
the hash, the SHA-1 hash.

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:35.750
And, you know, that's
something that we're

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:36.800
particularly proud of.

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:40.220
We also know that SHA-1's
not used extensively,

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:42.131
but it's not safe, right?

00:17:42.131 --> 00:17:43.880
And just by even
bringing that information

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:45.504
to the world, maybe
that scale, showing

00:17:45.504 --> 00:17:46.977
what it took to break it--

00:17:46.977 --> 00:17:48.060
it's an interesting paper.

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:49.010
I encourage folks to read it.

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:50.810
But it's one out of
many, many research

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.680
papers we've published.

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:54.500
And we engage with the
academic community,

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:59.650
and funded a lot of research
grants in security, as well.

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:03.590
I'll highlight, too, our
vulnerability rewards program.

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:05.517
We encourage folks
to break our system.

00:18:05.517 --> 00:18:07.100
We encourage Googlers
to do it, and we

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:10.460
encourage anyone in the
outside world to do so.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:13.790
And we motivate them with money
and fame, and some of them

00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:16.500
even get jobs at Google.

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:19.720
And it's been a wonderful,
wonderful program

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:22.460
of mutual benefit for
us, because if someone

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:23.960
does find a problem,
that's great

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:26.600
because we are able
to patch it and bring

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.620
that benefit to our customers.

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:30.830
And again, we've paid out
several million dollars

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:32.840
in vulnerability
rewards to date,

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:35.540
and continue to keep that going.

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:38.210
We have global site
reliability experts,

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:43.130
global incident management
protecting our sites and data.

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:45.935
And if anything occurs,
again, the turnaround in SLAs

00:18:45.935 --> 00:18:50.000
and incident management at
Google are unparalleled.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.700
And I mentioned that we manage
and write our own source code

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:54.500
and release management systems.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:59.456
We push 45,000 changes into
production every single day.

00:18:59.456 --> 00:19:00.080
Did you notice?

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:08.190
To be able to scale that many
changes takes a special system

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:11.780
to be able to manage over
2 billion lines of code

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:16.860
and to do so with low
to no disruptions.

00:19:16.860 --> 00:19:20.286
I challenge any on-premise
operation to meet that,

00:19:20.286 --> 00:19:22.660
and to be able to turn things
around as quickly as we do.

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:27.120
Deep data security.

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:29.310
Another thing that
separates Google

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:33.000
from the rest of the pack
is how we lay data down.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.110
First, we blow it up.

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:38.380
Blow it up into tiny shards.

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:41.670
We take each of those chance
of data and give them a key--

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:43.620
a chunk key.

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:46.090
Lay it down on disk.

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:48.190
In addition, we
take each of those

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:51.250
and we wrap them
in a wrapping key,

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.490
and that's hosted by our KMS.

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:57.897
That wrapping key never
leaves the KMS unencrypted.

00:19:57.897 --> 00:20:00.230
Now, after we've done that,
as if that's not hard enough

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:01.790
to really be able
to figure out OK,

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:08.720
if I need to reassemble this,
how do I go about doing that?

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:11.610
We rotate those keys
every 90 days, as well.

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:13.610
So both sets of
keys get rotated.

00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:16.010
So you can imagine a
gigantic paper shredder,

00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:18.530
each one of those shreds
of paper having a key,

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:21.860
and then there being a
separate key that manages

00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:24.350
all that information, as well.

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:30.360
Very, very hard
to reassemble data

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.610
unless you have all
of the information

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:35.070
and it's all verified, also
with an additional identity

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:38.310
layer every time someone
sources information.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:43.580
In addition, we're
the proud developers

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:47.510
of Chrome, world's
most beloved browser.

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:51.350
And it is the only browser
that can be managed entirely

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:51.980
from the cloud.

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:56.830
That gives admins in
the enterprise space

00:20:56.830 --> 00:21:00.100
tremendous power, and it
gives you a tremendous insight

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:01.450
into what's happening.

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:05.230
It also allows for wonderful,
wonderful interplay

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:08.380
and separation of
your work persona

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:10.037
and your personal personas.

00:21:10.037 --> 00:21:12.120
And to be able to move
back and forth between them

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:14.500
is something we
do within Google.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:16.000
We've worked with
a lot of customers

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:17.920
to show them how
that can be done,

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:21.730
and it's just a
phenomenal browser.

00:21:21.730 --> 00:21:24.750
And I'm not just the
only one saying that.

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:26.350
I hear that often
from our customers.

00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:31.600
Moving on to a
topic that we often

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:35.860
find since we're the
providers of Gmail--

00:21:35.860 --> 00:21:40.820
the topic of spam, phishing,
malware, and the like.

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:48.360
So we get about 0.1% of spam
in the average Gmail inbox.

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:50.020
That's a very, very,
very low number,

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:53.370
and we're always working
to make that even lower.

00:21:53.370 --> 00:22:01.840
And along false positives rates,
we get 0.05% of wanted mail

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:05.140
actually ending up
in the spam folder.

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:07.990
I know I may sound
biased, but I can't

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:09.370
think of the last
time I actually

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:15.320
had a valid message show
up in my spam folder.

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:17.030
Now, how do we do this?

00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:21.970
There is a session later today
on email, Gmail security,

00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:23.900
and so folks want
greater details,

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:26.510
I encourage them to
attend that session.

00:22:26.510 --> 00:22:29.030
But there are
basically three phases

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:31.640
in how we handle
the evaluation and

00:22:31.640 --> 00:22:33.590
the machine-learned
evaluation of what's

00:22:33.590 --> 00:22:36.780
come in to your mailbox.

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:40.900
Now, 90% of all spam is caught
at the, sort of, SMTP layer,

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:42.860
pre-acceptance.

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:46.130
But once we have it,
we have a robust set

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:51.690
of proprietary algorithms
informed by decades, almost,

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:55.710
of Gmail operations that do
things like reputation-scoring

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:59.800
and entity co-concurrence and
a whole bunch of other things.

00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:03.210
These algorithms
are world-class.

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:06.750
In fact, I see routinely
these days startups,

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:09.390
sort of coming to sell to
on-premise operators, who

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:11.550
sell one tiny
piece of that logic

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:17.534
that we've had in Gmail
for many, many, many years.

00:23:17.534 --> 00:23:21.150
And even after we've
delivered a message,

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:24.150
we'll do deep scanning
of attachments.

00:23:24.150 --> 00:23:26.730
Within four minutes-- and we
have this in Trusted Tester

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:27.330
right now--

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:29.860
that deep scanning of
an attachment occurs.

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:31.860
So we can be sure that
if you're about to launch

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:35.520
something or open it
up, it's going to be OK.

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:37.770
And we also do a lot of
click time evaluation.

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:40.387
So if you decide to
click a URL quickly,

00:23:40.387 --> 00:23:41.970
real-time we will
check whether or not

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:44.160
that URL is safe to launch.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.620
And there's a whole bunch
of HTML sanitization

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:48.750
and a whole bunch of
other things that go on.

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:51.330
I encourage folks, if you're
running on-premise operations

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:54.090
and you're talking to vendors
about individual solutions

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:56.370
to help you with spam
and phishing and malware,

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:58.360
there's a lot of great
providers out there,

00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:00.825
but be sure it's not redundant
with what we offer already.

00:24:04.360 --> 00:24:06.390
Now, we have layers
of secure messaging.

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:09.130
Beyond, sort of, spam phishing
and malware detection,

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:12.840
we've done a lot to help
with, for example, encryption.

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:15.460
Gmail now supports
S/MIME, which we're

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:19.480
particularly happy to do under
a bring-your-own certificates

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:21.350
model.

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:25.420
We just stopped blocking
JavaScript attachments.

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:29.470
And we launched last year-- many
of you may know about the red

00:24:29.470 --> 00:24:30.830
padlock--

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:34.030
a TLS indicator to show if,
at any point along the way,

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:36.400
your email was sent unencrypted.

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:40.450
That tiny padlock in the
course of three weeks

00:24:40.450 --> 00:24:45.100
changed the state of
encryption on the internet.

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:47.470
And there's great
examples of the way

00:24:47.470 --> 00:24:52.810
that Google innovates in a
noninvasive, helpful, friendly,

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:57.130
Googley manner that comes
back to our culture.

00:24:57.130 --> 00:25:00.190
And just says listen, I'm going
to give you the information

00:25:00.190 --> 00:25:01.720
and you can act
on it accordingly.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:03.610
And it's funny how the
self-organizing world

00:25:03.610 --> 00:25:05.837
of the internet said,
hey, now that everyone

00:25:05.837 --> 00:25:07.920
can see that I'm not
encrypting, perhaps I should.

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:15.480
We also launched
DLP with a whole lot

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:17.260
of prepackaged features.

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:19.140
For example, you know,
credit card numbers,

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:22.230
license plate
numbers by country.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:24.120
Algorithms based on
by-country things

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:25.620
that allow you to
get sort of get up

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:28.290
and running really quickly
with prepackaged rules

00:25:28.290 --> 00:25:34.650
as well as custom rules, regular
expressions and the like.

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:38.620
Common images, even, are scanned
and analyzed through DLP.

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:42.450
Now, we offer DLP
in Gmail and Drive,

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:45.120
and the rules and
adoptions have been great.

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:48.480
We also are looking into
providing even more information

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:50.640
to customers about
the adoption of DLP,

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:52.150
what DLP's finding [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:52.150 --> 00:25:54.495
And stay tuned for more
information in that space.

00:25:58.910 --> 00:26:02.660
You can now export
Gmail logs to BigQuery.

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:07.940
This was a long-requested
feature from our customers.

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:11.090
Basically, we had the logs.

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:12.400
Our customers requested them.

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:14.650
It allows them to do a lot
more analysis on their own,

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:16.670
retain them for whatever
period they wish.

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:18.665
We're obliged by our own
regulatory obligations

00:26:18.665 --> 00:26:21.450
and the like to keep data
for certain amounts of time.

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:23.300
In this particular
case, customers

00:26:23.300 --> 00:26:27.030
can tweak that or reflect
their own rules and regulations

00:26:27.030 --> 00:26:28.490
and export the
information if they

00:26:28.490 --> 00:26:31.940
wish, as well, to other tools
for analytics like Data Studio,

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:33.220
Tableau, et cetera.

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:38.460
OAuth.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:43.989
So I've talked a lot about a
lot of these, sort of, what

00:26:43.989 --> 00:26:45.155
I call first party features.

00:26:47.659 --> 00:26:50.200
Let's see if I missed any that
maybe folks might want to know

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:51.300
a little bit more about.

00:26:51.300 --> 00:26:55.150
I mean, you know, there's
OU-based sharing settings,

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:57.550
Drive IRM.

00:26:57.550 --> 00:27:00.440
All of those things I'm happy
to discuss in greater depth

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:01.540
if folks want.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:05.140
But in addition, we now have
available in Trusted Tester

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:08.820
the ability to limit your OAuth
scopes and whitelist apps.

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:10.600
This is another feature
that not only have

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:13.720
our customers requested, but
we drink our own Kool-Aid

00:27:13.720 --> 00:27:14.560
at Google.

00:27:14.560 --> 00:27:16.960
We use all of our
applications, and, in fact,

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:19.790
google.com is one of
the biggest customers.

00:27:19.790 --> 00:27:22.180
So we have requested
this, as well,

00:27:22.180 --> 00:27:24.561
and it's a really
important feature--

00:27:24.561 --> 00:27:26.560
one that we're proud to
bring to Trusted Tester.

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:28.685
And I encourage you to talk
to your account manager

00:27:28.685 --> 00:27:31.330
if it's something that
you're interested in,

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:34.540
or come to the meet the
product manager session.

00:27:34.540 --> 00:27:37.114
Third floor of Moscone, I
think, from 4:00 to 6:00.

00:27:37.114 --> 00:27:39.280
Everyone's invited to come
talk to product managers.

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:47.660
Mobile device
management, so MDM.

00:27:47.660 --> 00:27:50.470
This is something that folks
have been asking for, as well,

00:27:50.470 --> 00:27:52.130
for quite some time.

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:55.090
It's evolved tremendously
in the last few years.

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:58.090
And the ability to go mobile
is fundamental to any operation

00:27:58.090 --> 00:28:01.940
anymore, and if you're managing
on-premise, mobile devices,

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:05.050
tablets, home device, bring
your own device, all of that,

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:07.570
it becomes complicated quickly.

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:09.790
We all know that
employee happiness

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:12.260
is rooted in the ability
to, sort of, hey,

00:28:12.260 --> 00:28:13.500
I'd like to use my own phone.

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:15.910
I'm an iPhone user,
I'm an Android user.

00:28:15.910 --> 00:28:20.200
People don't like to be forced
into certain technologies.

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:23.470
MDM actually helps you manage
a wide variety of devices

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:26.710
and ensure that your company's
policies are enforced no matter

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:29.350
what device they bring.

00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:30.880
Again, something
we use at Google,

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:34.660
and we allow our employees
to use various devices

00:28:34.660 --> 00:28:39.250
provided that the policies
are enforced on the device.

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:43.360
So also, it brings third
party app management,

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:46.810
more insight into your identity
and use, and the ability

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:49.120
to remote wipe.

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:51.230
I lost my phone.

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:53.060
Oh no, right?

00:28:53.060 --> 00:28:55.999
Now, what are you going to
do when you lose your phone,

00:28:55.999 --> 00:28:58.040
especially if it has a
lot of sensitive corporate

00:28:58.040 --> 00:28:58.700
information?

00:28:58.700 --> 00:29:00.680
Yeah, the phone may be
encrypted and the like,

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:02.840
but does somebody
actually have access

00:29:02.840 --> 00:29:04.640
to your password,
your passcode, or any

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:06.620
of those things on that device?

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:09.290
At times, unfortunately,
you do have

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:11.000
to wipe that device remotely.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:15.770
The ability to do that
easily with our solution

00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:19.237
is, again, something that
differentiates our solution

00:29:19.237 --> 00:29:20.320
from the rest of the pack.

00:29:24.590 --> 00:29:27.650
We're talking about devices.

00:29:27.650 --> 00:29:30.260
New sign-in from
Chrome on Mac here.

00:29:30.260 --> 00:29:33.660
So on a MacBook, you
get a notification

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:37.760
for the first time you're
logging into a new device.

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:39.860
But not limited to
just Chrome on Mac.

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:46.310
You can get that notification
on your mobile, and vice versa.

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:48.680
You can also get new mobile
notifications, right?

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:51.200
Your account spans
all of these devices.

00:29:51.200 --> 00:29:54.050
We do the work to
let people know

00:29:54.050 --> 00:29:56.180
what devices are being
used and what's happening

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:59.960
to your identity in the cloud.

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:05.790
And you can go into more
detail and understand, OK,

00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:07.700
was this me?

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:10.440
And if not, I'm going
to give you the chance

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:12.360
to secure your account.

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:16.230
Log yourself out of everywhere,
reset your password,

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:19.160
and get moving from there.

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:22.910
Very simple, very, very easy.

00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:24.810
It's what we're
dedicated to at Google.

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:27.240
We're dedicated to
taking the complexity out

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:32.420
of the things that make
on-premise operations so hard.

00:30:32.420 --> 00:30:34.690
So with that, I want
to talk about one

00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:38.410
of the innovations that
broadly separates Google

00:30:38.410 --> 00:30:40.390
from the rest of
the pack, and I'd

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:43.390
like to invite Reena
Nadkarni up to the stage.

00:30:43.390 --> 00:30:46.150
Reena's a great colleague,
senior product manager,

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:49.672
who's going to take us through
a demo of security keys.

00:30:49.672 --> 00:30:50.880
REENA NADKARNI: Hi, everyone.

00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:54.360
I'm Reena Nadkarni, product
manager on the G Suite team.

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:57.390
Today I'm going to tell you
about security keys, both

00:30:57.390 --> 00:31:01.920
from the end user side as
well as from the admin side.

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:05.740
If you can switch to
my laptop, please.

00:31:05.740 --> 00:31:10.170
So let's start with a
very simple guest browser,

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:13.260
and we're going to look to
see what the experience looks

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:16.620
like if you need to register
for a new security key.

00:31:16.620 --> 00:31:31.335
So I'm just going to logging
in with my test account user.

00:31:31.335 --> 00:31:32.585
It's asking me for a password.

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:42.360
And what you're seeing
here is that the admin

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:46.164
has required this domain
to have a security key.

00:31:46.164 --> 00:31:48.330
And we'll talk a little bit
about the admin features

00:31:48.330 --> 00:31:50.210
that we are providing shortly.

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:51.960
But here, it's asking
me, hey, do you want

00:31:51.960 --> 00:31:53.789
to register a security key?

00:31:53.789 --> 00:31:55.080
Let's say I'm going to say yes.

00:31:57.689 --> 00:31:59.230
And obviously, for
security purposes,

00:31:59.230 --> 00:32:00.730
it needs my passport again.

00:32:06.062 --> 00:32:08.270
And so now it's saying, do
you have your security key

00:32:08.270 --> 00:32:08.670
with you?

00:32:08.670 --> 00:32:10.110
And I have my
security key, which

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:13.095
was given to me by my admin.

00:32:13.095 --> 00:32:15.390
You know, at Google, we
have them at our tech store.

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:18.120
We say you should literally
have these in a candy

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:20.070
bowl in every break room.

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:24.510
So I say yes, I have my
security key with me.

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:25.430
I click Next.

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:29.710
And it's telling me
to just plug it in.

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:31.420
And then when the
security key blinks,

00:32:31.420 --> 00:32:37.180
I just have to tap
on it, and there--

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:39.590
this security key is registered.

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:42.980
This has associated my
account, my Gmail account,

00:32:42.980 --> 00:32:45.980
with the security
key one-one-one.

00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:48.410
And when I click Done
here, it tells me

00:32:48.410 --> 00:32:50.580
that a security
key was registered.

00:32:50.580 --> 00:32:53.140
It talks about when
it was registered.

00:32:53.140 --> 00:33:05.060
And if I now go back
to my mail, it's

00:33:05.060 --> 00:33:09.230
also going on notify me, saying,
hey two-step verification was

00:33:09.230 --> 00:33:11.490
turned on for your account.

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:13.490
So if you look at
what this does,

00:33:13.490 --> 00:33:16.430
this goes back to the
fundamental product principles

00:33:16.430 --> 00:33:17.750
we have at Google.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:19.880
We want security to
just work for you.

00:33:19.880 --> 00:33:21.560
We want it to not
get in the way,

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:24.660
because once it gets in the
way, people don't use it.

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:27.110
So that was kind of the
end user side of things,

00:33:27.110 --> 00:33:30.170
and how easy it is
to use security keys.

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:32.960
On the admin side, we've
added four new features

00:33:32.960 --> 00:33:34.730
for security keys--

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:38.690
enforce, enroll,
recover, and report.

00:33:38.690 --> 00:33:41.750
So if you think about enforce,
this is what you saw--

00:33:41.750 --> 00:33:44.790
the notification when I
first came in here saying,

00:33:44.790 --> 00:33:47.030
hey, you need to
register a security key.

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:50.390
This is because through the
admin console, the admin said,

00:33:50.390 --> 00:33:51.500
you have up to a week.

00:33:51.500 --> 00:33:53.700
It's a configurable
threshold of time.

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:55.240
And so that is enforce.

00:33:55.240 --> 00:33:57.620
Enroll is that
self-enrolling process

00:33:57.620 --> 00:33:59.450
that we just went through.

00:33:59.450 --> 00:34:00.770
And then you have recover.

00:34:00.770 --> 00:34:04.040
So many times, users sometimes
may lose their security key

00:34:04.040 --> 00:34:05.900
or it gets lost or stolen.

00:34:05.900 --> 00:34:09.619
Now, admins can do this at scale
where they can just, you know,

00:34:09.619 --> 00:34:13.170
revoke that security key and
the user can register a new one.

00:34:13.170 --> 00:34:16.010
And finally, to be able to
monitor the usage of security

00:34:16.010 --> 00:34:18.139
keys, and make sure
that they're sending out

00:34:18.139 --> 00:34:21.690
appropriate communications
to their entire domain,

00:34:21.690 --> 00:34:24.560
you can report on the usage
of these security keys

00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:27.090
using our admin console.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:29.480
So in short, security
keys are great.

00:34:29.480 --> 00:34:31.460
They're simple,
they're easy to use,

00:34:31.460 --> 00:34:34.340
and they protect
you from phishing.

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:37.130
At Google, we use
them every day,

00:34:37.130 --> 00:34:39.469
and I encourage you to come
to some of our more detail

00:34:39.469 --> 00:34:42.350
sessions tomorrow where we
will be giving away some

00:34:42.350 --> 00:34:44.840
of the security keys, as well.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:47.090
With that, I'm going
to call Suzanne back

00:34:47.090 --> 00:34:50.570
on stage to continue the
conversation on trust.

00:34:50.570 --> 00:34:52.449
Thank you.

00:34:52.449 --> 00:34:55.179
SUZANNE FREY: Thank
you, Reena, very much.

00:34:55.179 --> 00:34:58.930
Now, I mentioned that security
keys strongly differentiate us.

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:01.210
Google, along with the
FIDO Alliance and GitHub

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:04.810
all got together to
develop this security key.

00:35:04.810 --> 00:35:09.160
What makes this different than
traditional second-factor auth,

00:35:09.160 --> 00:35:11.980
or, like, say, one-time
tokens and the like

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:15.320
is that no code is
actually passed.

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:19.650
So there's no man in the
middle opportunity for this.

00:35:19.650 --> 00:35:22.164
So someone gets your
corporate credentials.

00:35:22.164 --> 00:35:23.830
They can't get the
corporate credentials

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:25.720
and then figure out
what the check sums,

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:28.660
what they're for in OTP,
or something like that.

00:35:28.660 --> 00:35:30.970
They actually need
the security key

00:35:30.970 --> 00:35:32.740
and your corporate
credentials to be

00:35:32.740 --> 00:35:35.200
able to hack into your account.

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:38.910
We have had exceptional,
exceptional results

00:35:38.910 --> 00:35:42.680
at Google with Googlers
using security keys.

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:45.820
If I were to tell our customers
one thing and one thing

00:35:45.820 --> 00:35:50.490
only that they needed to do
to improve their security,

00:35:50.490 --> 00:35:54.424
it is to adopt security keys
writ large within your company.

00:35:54.424 --> 00:35:56.840
And again, this is something
now that we're very proud of.

00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:58.298
Other companies
have come on board.

00:35:58.298 --> 00:36:00.520
Facebook, Salesforce,
and others have

00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:04.360
recognized that this is a
very, very strong security

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:05.060
technology.

00:36:07.970 --> 00:36:10.330
And I do encourage folks
to come get one tomorrow.

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:17.960
OK, so we've talked about
two other domains, right?

00:36:17.960 --> 00:36:21.350
We've talked about how
our security-first culture

00:36:21.350 --> 00:36:23.300
influences features,
it influences

00:36:23.300 --> 00:36:25.550
community engagement,
it influences

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:28.850
how we operate day in, day out.

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:30.950
We've talked about
vendor-in-the-middle

00:36:30.950 --> 00:36:34.850
elimination by building our
own stack and defense-in-depth,

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:38.520
and I want to finish up with
our strong track record.

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:42.240
Google was born in the cloud.

00:36:42.240 --> 00:36:47.190
From day one, we have optimized
for security and privacy

00:36:47.190 --> 00:36:48.900
in the cloud.

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:51.127
Have we done everything
perfectly for everything

00:36:51.127 --> 00:36:51.960
from the very start?

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:55.220
No, we haven't, but we
have made any mistakes

00:36:55.220 --> 00:36:58.070
and learned from them
extremely quickly,

00:36:58.070 --> 00:37:03.780
and it has made us
super strong today.

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:04.780
And we do this at scale.

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:11.130
And we have lots of outside
and independent parties

00:37:11.130 --> 00:37:15.030
verify that what we do
is what we say we do.

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:18.390
And we're proud to have the
badging of, for example,

00:37:18.390 --> 00:37:21.255
ISO 27000, 27017, and 27018.

00:37:21.255 --> 00:37:24.800
We're among the first
to get these badges.

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:27.730
We're FedRAMP-certified
for G Suite.

00:37:27.730 --> 00:37:32.330
Really important, really
onerous, and really,

00:37:32.330 --> 00:37:34.300
really this is one of
our proudest moments

00:37:34.300 --> 00:37:36.250
to achieve that certification.

00:37:36.250 --> 00:37:39.280
Something that if you can
hit the FedRAMP obligations,

00:37:39.280 --> 00:37:41.530
you can pretty much
hit everything.

00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:43.300
And the reporting
obligations to make sure

00:37:43.300 --> 00:37:46.992
that we're consistently
compliant are strict.

00:37:46.992 --> 00:37:49.450
And I'm proud to see some of
our FedRAMP folks in the room,

00:37:49.450 --> 00:37:50.950
and grateful to everything
that they've done.

00:37:50.950 --> 00:37:52.890
And grateful for
everything they've done.

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:54.640
And, of course, we
share our SOC reports--

00:37:54.640 --> 00:37:55.660
SOC 2, SOC 3.

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:58.240
We do our SOC 1.

00:37:58.240 --> 00:38:00.640
External auditors
maintain all of this.

00:38:00.640 --> 00:38:02.470
We work with our
customers to share

00:38:02.470 --> 00:38:04.150
the results that we can share.

00:38:04.150 --> 00:38:08.800
And the fact that, for
example, your data is not

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:11.170
used-- your enterprise
data is never

00:38:11.170 --> 00:38:14.320
used for advertising purposes.

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:16.730
I want to repeat that.

00:38:16.730 --> 00:38:21.620
Enterprise data is not used for
advertising purposes at Google.

00:38:21.620 --> 00:38:24.560
It would violate the
covenants of our agreements

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:25.390
with our customers.

00:38:25.390 --> 00:38:27.167
It would violate our culture.

00:38:27.167 --> 00:38:29.750
It would violate everything that
we've committed to customers.

00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:33.249
But we get audited against
that to ensure folks

00:38:33.249 --> 00:38:34.040
know that, as well.

00:38:37.800 --> 00:38:39.890
In addition, we have data
protection compliance

00:38:39.890 --> 00:38:42.530
ingredients that we
commit to our customers.

00:38:42.530 --> 00:38:46.460
In our terms of service
outline, how we treat your data,

00:38:46.460 --> 00:38:48.620
we in addition have a
data processing amendment

00:38:48.620 --> 00:38:51.500
that shows specifically
what we do with your data

00:38:51.500 --> 00:38:53.360
once we have it.

00:38:53.360 --> 00:38:55.700
What our obligations
are if you delete it,

00:38:55.700 --> 00:38:59.264
how long we can take until
it's completely gone.

00:38:59.264 --> 00:39:01.180
And last but not least,
and we're very proud--

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:04.150
and I see Mark
here in the room--

00:39:04.150 --> 00:39:10.210
of achieving common opinion
MCC approval in the EU.

00:39:10.210 --> 00:39:11.570
What does that mean?

00:39:11.570 --> 00:39:14.040
That basically means
anyone in the EU

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:18.200
who wants to use G
Suite has the approval

00:39:18.200 --> 00:39:20.840
of their DPA in that country.

00:39:20.840 --> 00:39:23.720
You don't have to go through
extra hoops or one-off one,

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:26.550
and that's for
most EU countries.

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:30.300
It's a big, big, big
differentiator for us, as well.

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:36.540
So I talked about, I
think, a lot of the things

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:37.830
that some folks maybe know.

00:39:37.830 --> 00:39:41.730
Maybe they knew some of them,
maybe didn't know all of.

00:39:41.730 --> 00:39:45.570
But there's a lot of
hidden wisdom in the cloud,

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:48.300
and specifically
in Google's Cloud.

00:39:48.300 --> 00:39:51.030
The fact that we were
born in the cloud,

00:39:51.030 --> 00:39:55.440
and that we have a huge
user base, across consumer

00:39:55.440 --> 00:39:57.980
all the way through
to enterprise.

00:39:57.980 --> 00:40:01.880
We have a lot of people
watching our service.

00:40:01.880 --> 00:40:04.880
Look at an on-premise operation,
and you have your company.

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:07.550
You might have the good citizens
who are out there saying, hey,

00:40:07.550 --> 00:40:09.260
I see a bug.

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:12.790
Or I'm logging in and I'm
seeing something wrong.

00:40:12.790 --> 00:40:17.110
What happens when that's
tens, hundreds of millions

00:40:17.110 --> 00:40:20.620
plus individuals
using that service?

00:40:20.620 --> 00:40:24.660
You get near
instantaneous information

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:27.790
in a solution like that
about security and privacy

00:40:27.790 --> 00:40:29.860
vulnerabilities.

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:34.770
It's a huge advantage
of using the cloud.

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:36.900
In addition, I often
say that security comes

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:39.180
in multiple forms at the cloud.

00:40:39.180 --> 00:40:42.540
There's the obvious, sort of,
cybersecurity side of things,

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:45.810
and having the best experts,
protecting this information,

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:48.270
and that is the
reason that they show

00:40:48.270 --> 00:40:50.610
up for work every single day.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:54.120
But then there's also, sort
of, technical obsolescence

00:40:54.120 --> 00:40:56.650
or stagnation, right?

00:40:56.650 --> 00:40:59.480
Security is commercial
security, as well.

00:40:59.480 --> 00:41:01.630
And if your company is
actually constantly getting

00:41:01.630 --> 00:41:04.210
bogged down and can't innovate
quickly enough because they're

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:08.290
waiting for the next patch of
x, or the servers to arrive,

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:11.290
or the vendor to actually
get the right people in place

00:41:11.290 --> 00:41:15.340
to get the job done, that is
going to slow your company down

00:41:15.340 --> 00:41:20.530
to a rate that no company in
this environment can afford.

00:41:20.530 --> 00:41:24.340
So security comes in more
than one form in the cloud.

00:41:24.340 --> 00:41:26.320
And it's certainly
one of those forms

00:41:26.320 --> 00:41:30.040
is commercial agility,
the ability to innovate,

00:41:30.040 --> 00:41:31.720
and the ability
to take advantage

00:41:31.720 --> 00:41:34.300
of each and every
other customer's needs.

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:38.230
We get information inputs
from multiple sources

00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:41.200
all over the world, all
over different verticals,

00:41:41.200 --> 00:41:43.570
all over different customers.

00:41:43.570 --> 00:41:46.750
Those all get baked
into a common codebase,

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:49.660
and they get launched
across the world,

00:41:49.660 --> 00:41:51.730
and they get launched quickly.

00:41:51.730 --> 00:41:55.330
And that's another key
reason that the cloud

00:41:55.330 --> 00:41:58.150
is a wise, wise choice.

00:41:58.150 --> 00:42:00.970
No on-premise operation
benefits from that kind

00:42:00.970 --> 00:42:04.975
of scale of input and that
kind of scale of monitoring.

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:11.580
So in closing-- and we're going
to have some time for questions

00:42:11.580 --> 00:42:12.730
here--

00:42:12.730 --> 00:42:15.072
I just want to quickly
summarize again

00:42:15.072 --> 00:42:17.280
the three points I'd like
everyone to walk home with.

00:42:20.180 --> 00:42:23.475
On-premise operations tend not
to build their own servers,

00:42:23.475 --> 00:42:25.850
build their own data centers,
build their own application

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:28.460
services, have
their own networks,

00:42:28.460 --> 00:42:33.280
and that's probably fine for
someone small until it's not.

00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:35.460
And there's a vulnerability
or a patch needed by one

00:42:35.460 --> 00:42:39.010
of n number of vendors
that's actually

00:42:39.010 --> 00:42:40.810
put your company at risk.

00:42:40.810 --> 00:42:43.900
With Google, the
buck stops here.

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:47.620
We are accountable for the full
stack, and we have to fix it,

00:42:47.620 --> 00:42:50.380
because chances are, it's
not just one customer

00:42:50.380 --> 00:42:54.340
who's impacted, it's many.

00:42:54.340 --> 00:42:57.370
Second, security-first culture.

00:42:57.370 --> 00:42:59.500
It's not just our
security engineers.

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:03.070
It is every engineer, every
product manager in the cloud,

00:43:03.070 --> 00:43:07.190
that is responsible
for your data security.

00:43:07.190 --> 00:43:09.320
And everyone goes
through training.

00:43:09.320 --> 00:43:11.450
Everyone understands
that if we don't do that,

00:43:11.450 --> 00:43:14.570
everything else is not
even necessary because we

00:43:14.570 --> 00:43:17.200
got the security wrong.

00:43:17.200 --> 00:43:20.670
And we have strong
community engagement.

00:43:20.670 --> 00:43:22.830
And last but not least,
look at our track record.

00:43:26.557 --> 00:43:28.890
We've been protecting the
world's sensitive information,

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:32.380
whether under the banner
of enterprise or not,

00:43:32.380 --> 00:43:35.110
for well over a decade now.

00:43:35.110 --> 00:43:38.010
I'm proud to be
a part of Google.

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:40.980
I'm proud to have the trust
of the customers in this room.

00:43:40.980 --> 00:43:44.310
We all hope to have the trust
of our prospects, as well.

00:43:44.310 --> 00:43:47.370
I'm deeply humbled by the
opportunity to be a Googler

00:43:47.370 --> 00:43:49.500
and to serve the world
in this capacity,

00:43:49.500 --> 00:43:53.230
and to work around so many
great people who treat your data

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:56.150
with the utmost respect.

00:43:56.150 --> 00:43:59.120
And with that, I'd like to
say thank you and invite

00:43:59.120 --> 00:44:00.140
the room for questions.

00:44:00.140 --> 00:44:01.640
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:01.640 --> 00:44:05.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

